WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.603
Decompose into steps a person can review.

2
00:00:02.883 --> 00:00:19.949
Google’s engineering practices guide on small changes argues for keeping each change to one self-contained idea: small changes are reviewed faster and more thoroughly, are less likely to introduce bugs, are easier to roll back, and are less wasted work if rejected.

3
00:00:20.229 --> 00:00:24.155
It also suggests separating refactors from behavior changes.

4
00:00:24.435 --> 00:00:32.563
Every one of those reasons applies more strongly to agent-written code, because an agent can produce a large diff faster than you can read it.

5
00:00:32.843 --> 00:00:37.216
So break the work into steps where each step has its own check and its own commit.

6
00:00:37.496 --> 00:00:44.472
A useful default order: tests or fixtures first, then the core change, then integration and wiring, then cleanup.

7
00:00:44.752 --> 00:00:53.563
Keep mechanical changes (renames, moves, formatting) in separate commits from logic changes, so the logic diff is small enough to read.

8
00:00:53.843 --> 00:01:11.123
For long tasks, Anthropic’s post on harnesses for long-running agents describes what made multi-session work reliable: an explicit feature list kept in a file (they used JSON) with each item’s status, a progress file the agent updates, and git commits as checkpoints,

9
00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:16.395
with the agent working on one feature at a time and verifying it before marking it done.

10
00:01:16.675 --> 00:01:24.333
Their named failure modes are worth remembering: trying to one-shot the whole thing, and declaring victory before the work is verified.

11
00:01:24.613 --> 00:01:26.448
Those are management failures too.

12
00:01:26.728 --> 00:01:34.664
The fix in both worlds is the same: a written list of deliverables, one in progress at a time, and status backed by evidence.
